WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:03.679
Malcolm Grant: And then we move onto the business
plan. Bill, it is back to you.

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:08.440
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman. I would
like to introduce the business plan with a

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:14.889
short film that we put together. It tried
to capture a flavour of what matters to the

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:20.789
patients we serve, and it also has some contributions
from people around this table.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:27.789
I'd like patients to have a voice, but also
have a fairness to have them included. Not

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:41.500
just a number but they explain things to you.
Cause you need someone that's like, interested

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.660
as well to like know what you're going through
and give you advice as well. There's nothing

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:53.660
specific I was expecting them to do, just
get on with the work and hopefully everything

00:00:54.140 --> 00:01:01.140
works out ok.
To make sure that people are at the heart

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.950
of the health and care service in England
we've devoted our first business plan to

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.890
just that purpose. Our business plan says
that we will prioritise patients in every

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.999
decision we make, our objective is solely
to improve the outcome for patients and our

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:24.100
staff and their experience of health and care
in England.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:31.030
NHS England proposes to have a major focus
on quality. By quality I mean improving the

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:36.799
effectiveness of the care that we offer, ensuring
that it's delivered safely and that it is

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:42.740
convenient and decent for patients. We're
going to have a major focus on the things

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.880
which kill the majority of our population,
cardio vascular disease, cancer, lung disease

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:54.880
and liver disease. We're also going to focus
on better provision of care for long term

00:01:55.009 --> 00:02:01.049
conditions in such a way that we can encourage
patients to take greater control over the

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:08.049
diseases that they need help with.
I'm here for treatment for prostrate cancer,

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:15.710
I've got to go through 37 treatments which
is not daunting but it's worrying sometimes.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.660
I think they do a really good job at the moment
and I'm expecting them to continue to keep

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:35.310
up the good work.
We want good staff looking after people, like,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:42.310
good training. It's nice to see people actually
on the wards like, talking to people as sometimes

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:50.420
you just don't see them about.
NHS England puts patients first, it is absolutely

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:57.420
our priority and it runs through everything
we do. We want care to be provided that is

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:04.480
compassionate, that is based on high quality
outcomes and a fantastic patient experience.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.950
We are truly focused on making sure that happens
to all of the patients that we serve. Our

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:15.650
business plan commits to that in terms of
measuring care in that way and I introduced

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.880
last December the nursing strategy Compassion
in Practice that sets out what we will do

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.100
to improve care for patients.
This is Kieran, our youngest son, and that's

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:31.510
my husband John. I think when you come, you
don't want passing from pillar to post to

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.390
this department here to that department.
Our job is to make sure we deliver the best

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:40.690
possible health care for the money the tax
payer gives us but it's also to make sure

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:47.540
that we build the NHS to be sustainable for
the long term as we all age as a population.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.910
Both of those things get you to the same place,
we need people more engaged in their healthcare

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:56.190
and that means we need to have people who
are more engaged in the design of that healthcare

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:02.760
which genuinely meets the needs of the public.
We've got to create a visceral sense of

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.320
improvement and change and we've got to
create a visceral sense that the NHS and social

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.050
care system that health belongs to the people.
You have to start where people are at and

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.699
no matter how hard that is you have to value
their language, you have to help them understand

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:22.019
that they have power in the system. A lot
of people don't feel that they have any

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.749
power and they disengage and that leads to
misunderstanding about how they system actually

00:04:27.749 --> 00:04:31.550
works. Our legacy should be that health belongs
to the people.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:38.110
More communication and I'm going to say
resources but there is a lack of resources

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.389
and there is so much they can actually do
but as long as it's fairness and patients

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:47.400
are involved in their treatment, that we don't
make decisions without including those.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:54.400
I like people to explain things, I think even
now a lot of doctors, talk over you, use these

00:04:55.439 --> 00:05:02.439
big words and a nicer bedside manner. You're
very vulnerable when you come in hospital

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.830
and I think sometimes they take it, because
they're dealing with it day in day out it

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.719
might be your first experience. I think the
service is excellent, I think it would be

00:05:13.719 --> 00:05:19.069
a shame if we lost it.
To prioritise patients and citizens in the

00:05:19.069 --> 00:05:23.169
design and delivery of the health and care
service in this country means we have to listen,

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:27.990
we have to learn how to listen. The patient
revolution, the people revolution that we

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:33.699
are going to lead from now on is also an information
revolution and in the business plan we really

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:40.699
do make very radical new commitments to transparency
and information to support our ability to

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:47.800
properly listen to people as they go through
the health and care service.

00:05:54.629 --> 00:06:01.629
A great NHS would look like a customer at
the centre of the NHS I think what is deeply

00:06:15.159 --> 00:06:22.159
encouraging is that if everybody thought about
customers rather than systems and processes.

00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:36.520
We have to create a sense in which people
have ownership of process, so they understand

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.580
why the change happens and they're brought
in to designing and delivering services and

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.539
we have to create equality of access, treatment
and outcome.

00:06:43.539 --> 00:06:50.539
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman. Our business
plan is a response to that, to what the communities

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:57.029
we serve and the patients we serve demand
of us often at their most vulnerable and worried

00:06:57.029 --> 00:07:04.029
time. I think the plan we have before us today
is an ambitious one. It responds both to the

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:12.020
voice and aspiration of patients and communities,
but also is very important in the context

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:19.020
of the Robert Francis Report that we discussed
at the last Board meeting, and the expectations

00:07:19.219 --> 00:07:24.309
about cultural change and how we work into
the future.

00:07:24.309 --> 00:07:31.309
I want to draw out three areas of I think
quite significant ambition for us. The first

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:39.599
is around delivery; this is a plan which in
its 11-point scoreboard right at the front

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:50.229
sets out very openly how our success will
be judged, and also has the detail in the

00:07:50.229 --> 00:07:57.229
annex of how we are going to deliver the government's
mandate, and indeed our part in pushing through

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:05.249
the changes and improvements that everyone
counts, which we discussed in the autumn.

00:08:05.249 --> 00:08:12.249
It is very ambitious about delivery and improvement.
I think it is also very ambitious about fairness.

00:08:13.219 --> 00:08:18.770
I think we have been very consistent right
from the start around the table that it is

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:25.430
not good enough just to have overall improvement;
we need to be satisfied that those groups

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:31.619
who perhaps are marginalised or have been
disadvantaged are also getting the benefit

00:08:31.619 --> 00:08:38.070
of change and better health, better outcomes,
and a significant voice in the way we want

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:45.070
to shape and deliver services. We are making
a commitment in here to measure everything

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:53.380
we can not just by its aggregate improvement,
by what difference it is making to different

00:08:53.420 --> 00:09:00.420
groups in our communities. I would just point
out in paragraphs 102--105, in the third

00:09:01.259 --> 00:09:07.620
section, our interim equality objectives,
an important piece which as part of the business

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:14.620
plan we would like the Board to endorse today.
The third point of ambition I think I want

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:25.920
to set out is around our values and approach.
This comes straight back to what David was

00:09:29.399 --> 00:09:36.399
saying in the previous item. This is signposting
a different way to deliver change, improvement

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:45.220
and a new focus on priorities. Right at the
top of our scorecard, we are setting out the

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:54.490
two measures that we think are paramount above
all else. Those are satisfied patients (patients

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:02.940
who are experiencing the right sort of care
and service) and satisfied, empowered, motivated

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:10.589
staff. There is very strong evidence now that
those are umbilically linked. If we want to

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:15.949
improve outcomes, if we want to follow through
on the commitments for change, then we need

00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:21.630
to support our staff and give them the right
motivation, the right leadership, and the

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:27.560
right commitment and determination to do that;
the right things. We will be consistently

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:34.560
testing ourselves against how satisfied our
staff and patients are.

00:10:35.319 --> 00:10:42.250
We are also in here talking about a service
that is driven by patient and community voice.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:48.449
Again, this picks up a lot on the last item.
There have been charges, perhaps even in the

00:10:48.449 --> 00:10:53.779
Robert Francis Report, that too often the
service has been straining its neck upwards

00:10:53.779 --> 00:11:00.779
to understand what politicians are looking
for next. This is now the opportunity to face

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:06.610
outwards, to work with patients and communities
all the time, understanding what will make

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:11.870
the greatest difference to them, and using
that connection, that understanding, to drive

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:16.040
our priorities and what we believe is most
important.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:22.110
There is a lot in here about clinical leadership.
At the heart of some of these changes is that

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:28.250
belief that clinicians and particularly our
local clinical leaders in Clinical Commissioning

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:34.240
Groups can be closest to patients, can make
the changes that matter most to local communities.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:41.240
There is a whole piece in here about we as
NHS England standing alongside our local Clinical

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:47.240
Commissioning Groups together to bring about
the change that their communities are looking

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.509
for.
There is a great deal about transparency,

00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:56.199
our commitment to openness, which you, Chair,
have championed from the very start of the

00:11:56.199 --> 00:12:03.199
Commissioning Board Authority as it was then.
From those small beginnings, this is now setting

00:12:04.259 --> 00:12:11.259
out some serious ambitions, perhaps exemplified
by Bruce's work in publishing outcomes across

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:19.190
those ten surgical specialties.
Last but not least, I would point to a new

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:26.190
culture of partnership that runs through this
business plan. We have already developed very

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:31.690
good relations with the new parts of the system
that you talked about in the introduction.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:38.690
Also, I would flag up what I think is a burgeoning
partnership with local government. You and

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:45.209
I met with the Chair and Chief Executive of
a Local Government Association a few weeks

00:12:45.209 --> 00:12:51.769
ago, and I think we agreed there that we are
probably in a better place to build positive

00:12:51.769 --> 00:12:56.420
relationships, to make improvements around
integrated care for local communities, than

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:03.420
NHS and local government has ever experienced
before. We need to build on that to really

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:08.500
bring to life the Health and Wellbeing partnership
as a way to get great value out of limited

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:14.170
public resource, but to do that in a way that
responds to patients and communities locally.

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:21.100
An ambitious plan, hopefully one that really
will make a difference for the patients and

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.500
communities we serve. I would ask the Board
to note this plan.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:24.550
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Bill.
Let us take some comments from colleagues

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:28.740
on the Board. Naguib?
Naguib Kheraj: I think this is excellent.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:35.740
I fully endorse the direction and the principles.
I had two observations when I re-read this;

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:53.990
or three observations, I would say, actually.
One is that it does come across again like

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:08.670
an NHS policy document: 60 pages long and
by the time you have finished reading it,

00:14:09.399 --> 00:14:16.399
you ask yourself what is the big message of
it. There is a big message of it, and it is

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:34.490
right there up at the front. However, I think
it is very important that it does not become

00:14:45.579 --> 00:14:52.579
a 60-page document that is filed somewhere
and never referred to again as being a nice

00:14:53.709 --> 00:15:00.170
policy document business plan.
The two things that I think we need to focus

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:07.170
on tangibly are, if you look at all these
objectives, almost none of them are going

00:15:12.209 --> 00:15:19.209
to be addressed in one shot; they are evolution-over-a-period-of-time
objectives. Without wanting to get into a

00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:30.589
world that is focused on targets and numerical
targets again, these are all things that if

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:47.470
you are trying to measure progress on them,
you need to have some sense of, 'That is

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:57.290
a benchmark to shoot for, and then when we
get to that one, we are going to try to improve

00:16:07.839 --> 00:16:14.839
it to the next stage, so what are the steps
along the way that say that is good, that

00:16:20.499 --> 00:16:23.939
is better?' That is really where we are
trying to head.

00:16:23.939 --> 00:16:30.060
The two thing for me from that list of different
objectives were about, how do you create some

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:32.490
time-bound staging post to say, six months
from now, 12 months from now, 24 months from

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:38.660
now, where do we want to be on this journey?
Two is, how do you get to some measures that

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:42.509
allow you to say, are you succeeding or not?
A lot of the comments are not precise in definition.

00:16:42.509 --> 00:16:48.350
You can agree with the concept, but you would
not know if you are succeeding or not. I think

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:49.970
the only way to do that is to reduce it to
fewer things that we look at regularly in

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:50.220
evolution, with data, to say, are we travelling
in the right direction over certain periods

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:53.009
of time? Otherwise, what ends up happening
is a 60-page, nice document that expresses

00:16:53.009 --> 00:16:54.529
good intent, but how do you translate it into
some useful tool? I am not sure.

00:16:54.529 --> 00:16:56.100
Malcolm Grant: Shall I take one or two other
points, Bill, and you might want to come back

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:56.639
to address them. I have both Jo and Ed.
Jo-Anne Wass: Thank you. I just wanted to

00:16:56.639 --> 00:16:57.209
draw our collective attention to the bit of
the business plan that talks about us as an

00:16:57.209 --> 00:16:57.459
organisation, and our aspirations to be excellent.
Just to focus on how important the development

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.810
of our own organisation is and the experience
of our own staff in this, I think often it

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:05.400
has been my experience of working in the NHS
in the past that strategic organisations with

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:11.159
significant system-wide responsibilities tend
to look out to the system a lot, and can sometimes

00:17:11.159 --> 00:17:17.140
fall into the trap of mostly looking outwards
and not inwards. I think us being NHS England

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:17.419
as an employing body with 6,500 staff being
an excellent organisation is the foundation

00:17:17.419 --> 00:17:20.189
for the achievement of all the other stuff.
I would very much support what Margaret said,

00:17:20.189 --> 00:17:23.610
but would add to it that I think our staff
experience is just as important as experience

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:26.459
of staff on the frontline.
Ed Smith: I think it is excellent; I commend

00:17:26.459 --> 00:17:27.829
it. I think it particularly provides a bridge
between the mandate and what we have to do.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:34.750
I think Naguib's point is very well taken,
because I think this has to be a living document

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:41.080
in a number of ways. I think with my audit
committee, one of those is translating from

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:44.440
the business plan -- and I have already this
conversation with Bill and Paul -- into what

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.730
are the risks associated with the execution
of this plan, and what are the mechanisms

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.929
for keeping the Board apprised of the assurance
on those risks, the management of those risks,

00:17:51.929 --> 00:17:58.929
as we go through the course of the next year
or two. I think its utility is very much in

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:05.940
what we then do with it, and I would like
to see a very clear link between the delivery

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.460
of the business plan, the risks associated
with it, and the mitigations that management

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:22.460
have in train to make sure that we execute.
Victor Adebowale: I think it is great. I have

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:38.370
said so on film now, so it is a bit late.
I think this is a really good plan. I guess

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:54.610
the thing for me, agreeing with Naguib and
Ed absolutely, is we need to be really honest

00:18:55.480 --> 00:19:02.480
about the challenge that is represented in
this plan. This is a huge challenge. I think

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:09.760
one of my things is really about explaining
to people what this means; becoming an excellent

00:19:13.659 --> 00:19:19.850
organisation in relation to the equality issues
that we have talked about, the leadership

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:26.020
challenges. The idea of engaging communities
at a time when people will campaign not to

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:33.020
change anything is a big challenge. There
is almost a bit about this document, to make

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:44.710
it live, which is explaining or engaging with
people in discussion about, 'What does this

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:50.289
mean then? The implications of this mean that
actually, things are not going to stay the

00:19:50.289 --> 00:19:56.150
same; you will have to do things differently,
and what does that mean?' I think that is

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:59.429
what creates the, 'Wow, this is not just
a 60-page document; this means that I am doing

00:19:59.429 --> 00:20:05.640
something different on Monday that I was not
doing on Friday.' Getting people to explain

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:10.649
that to themselves and us would be really
powerful. I think this is great; it is a step

00:20:10.649 --> 00:20:15.260
forward.
Ciarán Devane: This really builds on Naguib.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:20.260
I think part of what we need to debate as
we go forward is what are we actually going

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:27.260
to do to improve things under Domain 1, 2,
3, 4 or 5, and make sure we are clear about

00:20:27.529 --> 00:20:29.620
what those few priorities that we will focus
on in the intermediate term are. The mandate

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:35.700
itself, but also this, is an excuse to do
everything, so it is really around saying,

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.960
'What will we land in three months' time,
in six months' time, in 18 months' time,'

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:43.250
and being clear on that, saying, 'Well,
we cannot do everything immediately.' Then

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:50.250
have the reporting mechanism back on those
as well, so that it is not just the numbers

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:54.429
overall and our progress that comes back,
but that we are confident we have the actions

00:20:54.429 --> 00:20:59.480
in place to drive progress over time.
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thanks. Bill, you may

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:03.390
wish to respond. Some of those are for absorption
rather than for response, but please, respond

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.980
if appropriate.
Bill McCarthy: Yes, they are, and I completely

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:12.980
agree with them. I think there is a risk on
the 60-pager, which in a sense is why we did

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:21.090
that little two-side plan-on-a-page upfront.
If anyone wants the potted version of what

00:21:23.169 --> 00:21:30.169
we are about, it is there on two sides. The
important accompanying piece is exactly the

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:37.580
one that Ed talked about in his contribution,
which is making sure that the Board assurance

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:44.580
and reporting framework is now wrapped around
this and is created in a way that brings to

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:51.149
the Board both those strategically important
measures, but also those that appear to be

00:21:51.149 --> 00:21:56.590
the riskiest and therefore that we should
put most attention to. That is the piece of

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:01.240
work, along with the Audit Committee, that
we are carrying out now.

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:08.240
I would also say that David took his senior
team away just before Easter to be thinking,

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:16.130
'What should we be spending our time on?'
The way he ran that was to look at the business

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:19.340
plan and start understanding where the risks
are, where the immediacy is, so that your

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:23.399
senior executive team is already beginning
to work through a risk-based approach to what

00:22:23.399 --> 00:22:30.399
we need to do to help deliver and support
all of our people to deliver the business

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:33.279
plan. The points are exactly made, and we
will be picking those up.

00:22:33.279 --> 00:22:39.720
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bill. On behalf
of the Board, can I commend you and your team

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:46.720
for the work that has gone into producing
such an excellent business plan? Within it,

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:56.640
it has both a spelling-out of the enormity
of the challenges that we face, but also I

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:00.720
think a sense of can-do and a sense of where
we go to in order to be able to deliver on

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:07.720
the five Domains for which we are accountable.
We look forward to further iterations. I am

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:15.929
very sympathetic to the idea that 65 pages
is a long read, but also quite sympathetic

00:23:15.929 --> 00:23:21.340
to the idea that to compress it into anything
smaller would be quite a challenge, because

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:27.059
there is so much to do. Naguib's point is
very well made, that we need to understand

00:23:27.059 --> 00:23:33.059
what are the key priorities, how we measure
the phases of delivery, and how we measure

00:23:33.059 --> 00:23:39.480
success within each phase as we go along.
Subject to that discipline, may I ask the

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:45.529
Board to approve the business plan, but with
particular reference also to the interim equality

00:23:45.529 --> 00:23:51.399
objectives that are set out, particularly
in paragraph 3.104 (which we have not touched

00:23:51.399 --> 00:23:56.409
on, although Bill did mention in his outline
and Victor touched upon in his comments).

00:23:56.409 --> 00:23:59.779
Are we happy to approve it with those in particular?
Thank you very much.

